   Elias Marrow had kept the light at Karrow Rock for thirty-one years, and in that time he had learned that the sea   ┃
   never repeated itself. It only seemed to.                                                                           ┃
                                                                                                                       ┃
   Each evening, an hour before sunset, he climbed the one hundred and twelve steps to the lantern room. He counted    ┃
   them without meaning to, the way other men count heartbeats. At the top he wiped the great lens with a soft cloth,  [37m█
[39m   checked the rotation mechanism, and waited for the sun to touch the horizon. Then he lit the lamp, and Karrow Rock  [37m█
[39m   spoke to the dark the way it had since 1887: two long flashes, one short, then a pause of twelve seconds.           [37m█
